  <body>
    <h1>Squire Reading Stack 1: Order Structure</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A Student Reading Handout for Squire School</div>

    <div class="instruction-box">
      <strong>How to Use This Reading</strong>
      <p>
        This handout is designed for Squires to learn the foundational history and moral structure of the Order. Please
        read this document carefully. You will use this information to participate in moderated discussions, complete
        your reading log, and prepare for your oral review and written assessments.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is the Order?</h2>
    <p>
      The Order is an organization that was built upon the virtues of honesty, courage, obedience, charity, and
      reverence. Throughout its history, it was guided by a desire to protect the vulnerable and serve others. As a
      Squire today, learning about the Order means understanding how proper discipline, good character, and truthful
      history shape a person into a responsible and helpful leader.
    </p>

    <h2>Why Was the Order Formed?</h2>
    <p>
      Following the First Crusade (1095–1099), many Christian pilgrims wished to travel to the Holy Land, especially to
      the city of Jerusalem, for religious devotion. However, the roads were long and incredibly dangerous. Because the
      local Crusader states were fragile, these travelers often faced great threats and lacked protection.
    </p>
    <p>
      To solve this problem, the Order was founded around the year 1119 or 1120 by a man named Hugh de Payns and his
      first companions. Their original mission was simple and focused on service: to protect Christian pilgrims
      traveling on the dangerous roads to Jerusalem. They did not form the Order to seek wealth or power, but to provide
      safety and charity to those who were weak.
    </p>

    <h2>A Religious Order, Not an Ordinary Army</h2>
    <p>
      It is very important to understand the difference between a religious order and an ordinary army. An ordinary army
      exists to fight battles, conquer territory, or seek military glory. A religious order, however, is centered on
      spiritual life, discipline, and regular worship.
    </p>
    <p>
      Historically, the Order’s daily routine was filled with prayer, charity, and almsgiving. Their purpose was moral
      and protective. Today, the Squire School is educational and charitable. It is not a military or paramilitary
      program. Squires are expected to practice respect, helpfulness, and discipline, rather than imitating or
      glorifying war.
    </p>

    <h2>Classes in the Order</h2>
    <p>
      In the historical Order, not everyone performed the exact same function. Having different roles helped the
      organization serve more effectively. There were three major classes:
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 25%">Class</th>
        <th>Historical Role and Description</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Knight</strong></td>
        <td>The historical mounted noble class, often holding primary leadership and protective duties.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Sergeant</strong></td>
        <td>Members who provided vital supporting and serving roles below the knightly class.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Chaplain</strong></td>
        <td>
          Members responsible for priestly duties, leading worship, and guiding the religious ministry of the Order.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>Offices in the Order</h2>
    <p>
      Beyond the different classes, the Order also relied on specific administrative and leadership roles called
      offices. These offices ensured that the Order functioned in an orderly and disciplined way.
    </p>
    <table>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 25%">Office</th>
        <th>Leadership Duty</th>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Grand Master</strong></td>
        <td>The chief leader and head officer of the entire Order.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Seneschal</strong></td>
        <td>A high-ranking administrative officer who supported the leadership and managed key operations.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Marshal</strong></td>
        <td>An officer primarily responsible for specific organizational and logistical duties.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Preceptor</strong></td>
        <td>A leader or teacher placed in charge of a specific local house, area, or group of members.</td>
      </tr>
    </table>

    <h2>Clothing, Symbols, and Discipline</h2>
    <p>
      Historically, members of the Order wore specific clothing, such as a mantle (a type of cloak), and used distinct
      symbols. These symbols were meant to remind them of their identity and their duty to God and to the pilgrims they
      protected.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, it is dangerous to focus on outward appearances without inner character. Discipline is always more
      important than display. Wearing a symbol means nothing if a person is proud, disobedient, or unkind. For a Squire,
      personal discipline, humility, and respectful behavior at home, at school, and in the chapter are the true marks
      of readiness.
    </p>

    <h2>Service Before Self</h2>
    <p>
      The principle of "service before self" means putting your duty and the care of others ahead of your own pride,
      comfort, or desire for attention. The early Order lived this out by guarding dusty roads so that travelers could
      walk safely. For Squires today, service before self means dependable, quiet charity. Whether it is a formal
      service project or simply helping someone in need, real character is proven by reliable, humble helpfulness.
    </p>

    <h2>Truth Before Legend</h2>
    <p>
      Because the history of the Order is so old and fascinating, people have invented many legends, myths, and exciting
      stories about it over the centuries. As a Squire, you are called to value "truth before legend." History must be
      based on evidence and real sources. Repeating stories without proof replaces truth with myth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Honesty is a core virtue of the Order. This means studying events accurately, distinguishing historical facts from
      legends, and having the courage to say "I do not know" when you lack evidence. Protecting the truth is just as
      important as protecting travelers.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>Words to Remember</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pilgrim:</strong> A person who travels a long distance for a religious purpose.</li>
      <li><strong>Mantle:</strong> A cloak or outer garment worn historically to show identity and duty.</li>
      <li><strong>Legend:</strong> A story repeated over time that is not supported by historical evidence.</li>
      <li><strong>Charity:</strong> Care, mercy, and practical helpfulness toward others.</li>
      <li><strong>Reverence:</strong> A deeply respectful attitude in worship, speech, and conduct.</li>
      <li><strong>Discipline:</strong> Orderly conduct, self-control, and obedience to rightful guidance.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Short Reading Review</h2>
    <p>
      Use these points to check your understanding of the reading. You should be able to explain these to your
      Schoolmaster:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The First Crusade created a situation where pilgrims traveled to Jerusalem but faced great danger on the roads.
      </li>
      <li>Hugh de Payns and his first companions formed the Order to protect those traveling pilgrims.</li>
      <li>
        The Order was a religious community based on prayer and discipline, not just an ordinary army seeking glory.
      </li>
      <li>The three main classes of the historical Order were Knight, Sergeant, and Chaplain.</li>
      <li>Key leadership offices included the Grand Master, Seneschal, Marshal, and Preceptor.</li>
      <li>Inner discipline, character, and proper conduct are far more important than outward clothing or symbols.</li>
      <li>"Service before self" means prioritizing charity and the needs of others over personal pride.</li>
      <li>
        Squires must practice "truth before legend" by relying on historical evidence rather than repeating unsupported
        myths.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </body>
</html>
